post_theorem_intro_text:
\begin{proof}
By Bézout's lemma, there exist $x,y\in\mathbb{Z}$ such that $x\left(a+b\right)+yab=\operatorname{gcd}\left(a+b,ab\right)$. Note that there exist coprime $k,l$ such that $a=k\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)$ and $b=l\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)$. Hence, \begin{align*}
xk\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)+xl\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)+ykl\left(\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)\right)^2=\operatorname{gcd}\left(ab,a+b\right).
\end{align*}
So, \begin{align*}
f\left(a,b\right)=xk+xl+ykl\operatorname{gcd}\left(a,b\right)=xk+xl+byk
\end{align*}
which is an integer.
\end{proof}
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition f is a surjection}
$f$ is a surjection.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
We effectively wish to show that for any $c\in\mathbb{N}$, there exists $\left(a,b\right)\in\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}$ such that $f\left(a,b\right)=c$. Unlike conventional proofs of directly invoking the fundamental theorem of arithmetic, we condition on $c^2=a+b\in\mathbb{N}$. Thereafter, we can choose $a=c$ and $b=c^2-c$ so that \begin{align*}
    f\left(a,b\right)=f\left(c,c^2-c\right)=\frac{\operatorname{gcd}\left(c^2,c^3-c^2\right)}{\operatorname{gcd}\left(c,c^2-c\right)}.
\end{align*}
Since $c\ge 1$, then $c^3-c^2\ge 0$ and $c^2-c\ge 0$. As such, $f\left(a,b\right)=c$ so $f$ is surjective.
\end{proof}

