post_theorem_intro_text:
\begin{theorem} \label{T2}
Let $C$ be a general tetragonal canonical curve of genus $g \geq 6$ with $g \equiv 0 \mod 2$, then
$$0 \subset N_{C/Y} \subset N_{C/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}}$$
is the Harder-Narasimhan filtration of $N_{C/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}}$.
\end{theorem}

In the next section we introduce preliminary results and conventions. In section $3$ we will use degeneration to prove that $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$ is stable for the general curve, this proves Theorem \ref{T1}. We also obtain the stability of the bundle $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}}$ as a corollary.

\begin{corollary}
Suppose that
$$Q = \mathbb{P}(\O_{\mathbb{P}^1} \oplus \O_{\mathbb{P}^1}(a) \oplus \O_{\mathbb{P}^1}(b))$$
where $0 \leq a,b \leq 1$. Let $H$ denote the class of $\O_{\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})}(1)$ while $R$ is the class of a fiber. Then for any $c > 0$ if $r=3c+a+b+2$ the linear series $\lvert H + cR \rvert$ embeds $Q$ as a smooth rational normal scroll in $\mathbb{P}^r$ such that $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^r}$ is stable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Set $g:=3c+a+b+3=r+1$ and observe that $\lvert E+cR \rvert$ defines an isomorphism of $Q$ onto the projective bundle
$$\mathbb{P}(\O_{\mathbb{P}^1}(c) \oplus \O_{\mathbb{P}^1}(a+c) \oplus \O_{\mathbb{P}^1}(b+c)) \subset \mathbb{P}^r$$
Therefore the class $4H^2+(8c-2g+10)HR$ contains smooth tetragonal canonical curves of genus $g$ whose canonical class is the restriction to $C$ of $E+cR$. It follows that $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^r} \rvert_C$ is stable so we conclude that $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^r}$ is also stable, since the restriction of a destabilizing subbundle $\mathcal{F} \subset N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^r}$ would destabilize $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^r} \rvert_C$.
\end{proof}

Furthermore since $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$ is a quotient of $N_{Y/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$ the proof of Theorem \ref{T1} gives a bound on the slope of subbundles $\mathcal{F} \subset N_{Y/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$. It turns out that this bound is already good enough to give the semi-stability of $N_{Y/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$, so the results of section $3$ will also prove Theorem \ref{T2}.

expanded_sketch:
Next we will use degeneration to prove that $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$ is stable for the general curve; this establishes the main theorem. Furthermore, since $N_{Q/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$ is a quotient of $N_{Y/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$, the proof in establishing the main theorem gives a bound on the slope of subbundles $\mathcal{F} \subset N_{Y/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$. This bound is enough to give the semistability of $N_{Y/\mathbb{P}^{g-1}} \rvert_C$, so the same results will also prove the following theorem.

\begin{theorem} \label{T2}
Let $C$ be a general tetragonal canonical curve of genus $g \geq 6$ with $g \equiv 0 \mod 2$, then
$$0 \subset N_{C/Y} \subset N_{C/\PP^{g-1}}$$
is the Harder-Narasimhan filtration of $N_{C/\PP^{g-1}}$.
\end{theorem}

