ref_dict.main B:
\begin{thm}\label{main B}
(i) For any rank 2 polystable non-unitary Higgs bundle, its isomonodromic deformation over $\mathcal T_g$ is never holomorphic.\\
(ii) For any rank 3 stable non-unitary Higgs bundle, its isomonodromic deformation over $\mathcal T_g$ is never holomorphic (for $g\geq 3$, this statement also holds for any rank 3 polystable non-unitary Higgs bundle).
\end{thm}

ref_dict.main A:
\begin{thm}\label{main A}
For a generic Higgs bundle in $\mathcal M_{\text{Dol}}(\mathcal X/\mathcal T_g)$, the isomonodromic deformation is not holomorphic along any direction in $\mathcal T_g$.
\end{thm}

post_theorem_intro_text:
We note that Theorem \ref{main A} corresponds to the claim in \cite[Theorem 3.3]{biswas}. Their proof relies heavily on \cite[Theorem 1.6]{Tos}. Here, we present a different and simpler proof utilizing the criterion (\ref{holcri}) alongside properties of the associated spectral curve.

We also establish non-existence results for low-rank non-unitary Higgs bundles, specifically for ranks 2 and 3.
\begin{thm}\label{main B}
(i) For any rank 2 polystable non-unitary Higgs bundle, its isomonodromic deformation over $\mathcal T_g$ is never holomorphic.\\
(ii) For any rank 3 stable non-unitary Higgs bundle, its isomonodromic deformation over $\mathcal T_g$ is never holomorphic (for $g\geq 3$, this statement also holds for any rank 3 polystable non-unitary Higgs bundle).
\end{thm}
Theorem \ref{main B} (i) was first stated in \cite[Theorem 3.4]{biswas}. However, by applying the criterion (\ref{holcri}), our approach offers a simpler proof. Indeed, we show that $\theta_\ast$ is always non-zero under the assumptions of Theorem \ref{main B}.

Motivated by Theorem \ref{main A} and Theorem \ref{main B}, we make the following conjecture:
\begin{conjx}For a non-nilpotent Higgs bundle $(E,\bar\partial,\theta)$ in $\mathcal M_{\text{Dol}}(\mathcal X/\mathcal T_g)$, its isomonodromic deformation fails to be holomorphic over $\mathcal T_g$.
\end{conjx}
\vspace{.5cm}
\noindent\textbf{Acknowledgements:}
We extend our thanks to H\'el\`ene Esnault and Moritz Kerz for drawing our attention to the work in \cite{biswas}. We also thank Xin L\"u for valuable discussions. We are grateful to Jinbang Yang for helpful discussions on Appendix B.

