theorem.content:
[Inverse trace inequality for polynomials orthogonal to lower-order subspaces]\label{Theorem:inverse trace inequality}
For a planar $d$-dimensional simplical domain $T$ the following estimate holds for all $\xi \in \mathbb{P}_p({T})$ with $n \leq p$, such that:
\begin{align}\label{eq:sharp_estimate}
\|\xi - \Pi^{n}_{T}\xi\|^2_{L^2({F})}
\leq \frac{(p-n)(p + n+1 + d)}{d} {\frac{|F|}{|T|}}
\|\xi - \Pi^{n}_{T}\xi\|^2_{L^2(T)},
\end{align}
where ${F}$ denotes a $(d-1)$-dimensional face of $T$ and $\Pi^{n}_{T}$ denotes the $L^2$-orthogonal projection onto the $\mathbb{P}_n({T})$.  For $n=-1$, we set $\Pi^{-1}_T \xi := 0$.

post_theorem_intro_text:
On the other hand, if we applying  classical inverse trace inequality from \cite{warburton2003constants} directly to $\theta : = \xi - \Pi_T^{n}$, with $\theta = \xi - \Pi_T^{n}\xi \in \mathbb{P}_p(T)$, we infer
\begin{align*}
\|\theta\|_{L^2(F)}^2 \le \frac{(p+1)(p+d)}{d}\frac{|F|}{|T|}\|\theta\|_{L^2(T)}^2.
\end{align*}
However, recognizing that $\theta$ is orthogonal to $\mathbb{P}_{n}(T)$ which removes the contribution of the low-order polynomial modes. Consequently, the analysis provides the sharper bound
\begin{align*}
\|\theta\|_{L^2(F)}^2 \le \frac{(p-n)(p + n+1 + d)}{d}\frac{|F|}{|T|}\|\theta\|_{L^2(T)}^2.
\end{align*}
It is easy to see that the new inverse trace inequality bound is alway strictly than the bound using \cite{warburton2003constants} for $0\leq n \leq p$. For the case $n=-1$, the new bound will be equal to the other bound.
\par
\noindent
The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:notation} introduces notation and preliminary concepts. Section~\ref{sec:1D} presents one-dimensional proof,  while Sections~\ref{sec:2D} extend the analysis to triangles. In Section \ref{sec:3D}, we generalize the result to tetrahedron and also $d$-dimensional simplex.

